---
title: Two-step verification and account security
description: Set up a passkey or an authenticator app, keep recovery codes, sign in with two-step verification, and change your password.
sidebar:
  order: 3
---

Two-step verification asks for a second proof after your password, so that a stolen password alone is not enough to sign in. It is required for tenant admins and recommended for everyone.

All of it is on one page: select your avatar › **Settings** › **Account security**. The page lists **Passkey**, **Authenticator app**, **Password sign-in** and **Password**, each with its state and a **Set up**, **Manage** or **Change** button.

## Choose a method

| Method | How it works | Best for |
|---|---|---|
| **Passkey** | Your device confirms it is you with a fingerprint, face or PIN. A passkey also lets you sign in without a password, and it cannot be phished. | Computers and phones that support passkeys. |
| **Authenticator app** | An app on your phone (for example Google Authenticator, Microsoft Authenticator or 1Password) shows a six-digit code that changes every 30 seconds. | Devices that cannot use passkeys. |

An account uses one method at a time. To change from one method to the other, turn two-step verification off and set up the other method. There is no code by e-mail or SMS.

**Recovery codes** are the fallback when you cannot use your passkey or your app. There are 10 codes in a set, and each works once.

{% aside type="caution" title="Tenant admins must use two-step verification" %}
A tenant admin can create users, reset their passwords and act on their behalf, so a password alone is not enough. When a tenant admin without two-step verification signs in, Operon shows **Administrator accounts must use two-step verification**, and the rest of Operon cannot be used until a passkey or an authenticator app is set up. When it is done, select **Home** in the side bar to continue.

We recommend that a firm has at least two tenant admins, so that each can reset the other's two-step verification if a device is lost.
{% /aside %}

## Set up a passkey

{% steps %}
1. On **Account security**, select **Set up** next to **Passkey**.
2. Select **Add a passkey**. Your browser or device asks you to create a passkey; confirm with your fingerprint, face or PIN.
3. The passkey is listed with the date it was added. To use it from another computer or phone too, select **Add another device**.
{% /steps %}

To rename a passkey, select the pencil next to it, type the name and select **Save**. To remove one, select the bin and confirm. Your only passkey cannot be removed this way: removing it would turn two-step verification off, which is a separate action (below).

## Set up an authenticator app

{% steps %}
1. On **Account security**, select **Set up** next to **Authenticator app**, then **Set up authenticator app**.
2. **1. Scan this with your authenticator app**: scan the QR code with the app. The entry appears in the app as StaffOnAI with your e-mail address. If you cannot scan it, select **Can't scan it? Type this key instead** and type the key into the app.
3. **2. Enter the code it shows**: type the six-digit code from the app and select **Turn on**.
{% /steps %}

## Keep your recovery codes

Right after you turn on two-step verification, create a set of recovery codes and keep it somewhere safe, away from your phone or computer.

{% steps %}
1. On the passkey or authenticator page, find **Recovery codes**.
2. Select **Generate new**, then **Replace them**.
3. Under **Save these now**, select **Copy all** and store the codes, for example in your password manager.
4. Select **I have saved these**.
{% /steps %}

The card shows how many codes remain. When two or fewer remain, Operon reminds you to generate a new set. A new set replaces the old one: the old codes stop working.

{% aside type="note" %}
Turning on two-step verification signs you out on all your other devices.
{% /aside %}

## Sign in with two-step verification

After you type your e-mail and password and select **Sign in**, the **Two-step verification** page opens. It must be completed within 5 minutes; after that, Operon asks you to sign in again.

| Method | What you do |
|---|---|
| Authenticator app | Type the six-digit code from the app in **Authentication code**. It is checked as soon as the sixth digit is typed. |
| Passkey | Select **Use your passkey** and confirm on your device. |
| Recovery code | Select **Use a recovery code instead**, type a code in **Recovery code** and select **Verify**. Each code works once. |

If you have lost your phone or passkey and have no recovery code left, ask a tenant admin to reset your two-step verification (**Admin › Users** › **Reset two-step**). Then sign in with your password and set it up again.

## Turn two-step verification off

On the passkey or authenticator page, select **Turn off** under **Turn off two-step verification**. Type your **Password** and confirm with your current code or your passkey. A tenant admin cannot work without two-step verification, so turning it off only makes sense to change to the other method.

## Sign in with a passkey only

When you have a passkey, you can turn password sign-in off, so that your account can only be opened with the passkey: select **Password sign-in** on **Account security**, then **Turn password sign-in off**, and confirm with your password and your passkey. To allow it again, select **Allow password sign-in again** and confirm with your passkey.

## Change your password

{% steps %}
1. On **Account security**, select **Change** next to **Password**.
2. Type the **Current password**, the **New password** (at least 8 characters) and **Confirm password**.
3. Select **Confirm**. If two-step verification is on, confirm with your current code or your passkey.
{% /steps %}

After the password is changed, you are signed out on all your other devices.
